                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those ranges are typical nameplate figures, not measurements of your appliances; check your own labels or our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a>. The pattern holds for most homes, though. A 5,000 watt generator runs the fridge, freezer, furnace and sump pump together with margin. The window AC fits if you are not also starting the well pump. Central air and an electric water heater are out, and no amount of load management changes that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two conditions make this work. First, the generator must have enough running watts on the fuel you use, which at this size varies more than the labels suggest. Second, it needs a 240V outlet and a transfer switch to reach hardwired loads like the furnace, the sump pump on its own circuit and any 240V well pump. A 5,000 watt generator with only 120V outlets is a very good camping generator and a limited home backup one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Fridge, freezer, furnace, sump pump and lights: yes. Central air or a well pump plus AC: no.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 5,000 watt generator carries the essentials together and the bigger motor loads one at a time. If you want them all at once, the next useful size is around 8,000 watts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

