        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What will a 5000 watt generator run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A refrigerator, chest freezer, gas furnace blower, sump pump, lights and electronics at the same time for most homes, which typically totals 2,500 to 3,500 running watts. It can add a window air conditioner or start a 1 HP well pump, but usually not both together. It will not run central air or an electric water heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 5000 watt generator run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can run the essentials of most houses through a transfer switch, if it has a 120/240V L14-30R outlet. You will need to rotate the bigger loads, such as the freezer, well pump and window AC. It cannot run central air, an electric range or an electric water heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 5000 watt generator run a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes for a 1 HP or smaller pump, if the generator has a 240V L14-30R outlet and a transfer switch feeds the pump circuit. A 1 HP pump typically needs 3,000 to 4,000 watts to start, so start it before the window AC, not at the same time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 5000 watt generator enough for a refrigerator and freezer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Easily. A refrigerator and chest freezer together typically run at 1,000 to 1,400 watts, with brief starting surges around 2,000 watts. A 5,000 watt generator runs both with the furnace and lights, and still has room for a sump pump or a window AC.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 5000 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 9 to 10.5 hours at half load for the open-frame models here, based on manufacturer ratings, and up to 13.5 to 18 hours at light loads. At half load expect to burn about 0.4 to 0.5 gallons an hour, or around 6 to 10 gallons a day depending on hours run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 5000 watt inverter generator worth it for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it has 240V. Most 5,000 watt inverters, like the Westinghouse iGen5000DFcv, are 120V only with a TT-30R RV outlet, which cannot feed a home transfer switch for 240V loads. The WEN DF500iX is an exception with 120/240V output. For home-first use, an open-frame unit with an L14-30R gives more running watts per dollar.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a 5000 or 8000 watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy 5,000 watts if you are on city water with a gas furnace and are willing to rotate the freezer and window AC. Buy 8,000 watts if you are on a well, want air conditioning without managing loads, or want everything on the transfer switch to run at once.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
